        ":question/id": "03ad73f2-dfe8-5b7e-bde4-05067dff734f",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{2}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-2{x}^{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -5{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-2{x}^{3}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-2{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & =-6{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-2{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""
      },
      "role": "independently authored inventory",
      "relationship_to_source_templates": "Matches the power_moderate signed nonunit power verification family at a different coefficient/exponent, with the current three-checkbox contract and previously corrected interval wording; it is inventory, not recovered original MA difficulty evidence."
